@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "UNIQUE Constraint";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->
    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            UNIQUE Constraint
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            The UNIQUE constraint is used to ensure that all values
            in a column are different. It prevents duplicate values
            from being stored in a column.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- WHAT IS UNIQUE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is a UNIQUE Constraint?
        </h2>

        <p>
            A UNIQUE constraint ensures that no two rows contain the
            same value in the specified column.
        </p>

        <p>
            It is commonly used for columns such as Email, Phone
            Number, Username or other values that should remain unique.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Simple Example
            </h5>

            <p class="mb-0">
                If Email has a UNIQUE constraint, two students cannot
                have the same email address.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- UNIQUE RULES -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            UNIQUE Constraint Rules
        </h2>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Duplicate values are not allowed.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                UNIQUE constraints maintain data uniqueness.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                A table can have multiple UNIQUE constraints.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                A UNIQUE constraint can be applied to one or more columns.
            </li>

            <li>
                A UNIQUE constraint is different from a PRIMARY KEY.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- CREATE TABLE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Create UNIQUE Constraint
        </h2>

        <p>
            A UNIQUE constraint can be created while defining a table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    Email VARCHAR(150) UNIQUE
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- NAMED UNIQUE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Named UNIQUE Constraint
        </h2>

        <p>
            We can also provide a specific name to the UNIQUE constraint.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    Email VARCHAR(150),

    CONSTRAINT UQ_Students_Email
    UNIQUE (Email)
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- INSERT VALID DATA -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Insert Unique Values
        </h2>

        <p>
            Different email addresses can be inserted successfully.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
VALUES (101, 'Rahul', 'rahul@gmail.com');

INSERT INTO Students
VALUES (102, 'Priya', 'priya@gmail.com');</code></pre>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-success border-0">

            <strong>Result:</strong>
            Both records are inserted because their Email values
            are different.

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- DUPLICATE VALUE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Duplicate Value Example
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose the email already exists in the table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
VALUES (103, 'Amit', 'rahul@gmail.com');</code></pre>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-danger border-0">

            <strong>Error:</strong>
            The INSERT operation fails because the Email value already
            exists in the table.

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ADD UNIQUE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Add UNIQUE Constraint to Existing Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            A UNIQUE constraint can be added to an existing table
            using ALTER TABLE.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>ALTER TABLE Students
ADD CONSTRAINT UQ_Students_Email
UNIQUE (Email);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- MULTIPLE UNIQUE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Multiple UNIQUE Constraints
        </h2>

        <p>
            A table can contain multiple UNIQUE constraints.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Users
(
    UserId INT PRIMARY KEY,

    Username VARCHAR(50),

    Email VARCHAR(150),

    CONSTRAINT UQ_Users_Username
    UNIQUE (Username),

    CONSTRAINT UQ_Users_Email
    UNIQUE (Email)
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- OUTPUT -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example Output
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Email</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>rahul@gmail.com</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>priya@gmail.com</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Every Email value is unique in the table.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DIAGRAM -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            UNIQUE Constraint Structure
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL Server
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>
                                                            Students Table
                                                                  |
                                                                  ↓
                                                             Email Column
                                                                  |
                                                                  ↓
                                                            UNIQUE Constraint
                                                                  |
                                                                  ↓
                                                            Duplicate Values
                                                                 ❌
                                                                  |
                                                                  ↓
                                                             Unique Values
                                                                 ✓</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- PRIMARY KEY VS UNIQUE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            PRIMARY KEY vs UNIQUE
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>PRIMARY KEY</th>
                        <th>UNIQUE</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Uniquely identifies each row.</td>
                        <td>Ensures values are unique.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Only one PRIMARY KEY per table.</td>
                        <td>Multiple UNIQUE constraints can exist.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Does not allow NULL values.</td>
                        <td>NULL handling is different from PRIMARY KEY.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Usually identifies the main record.</td>
                        <td>Used for additional unique values.</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT POINTS -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Important Points
            </h5>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    UNIQUE prevents duplicate values.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Multiple UNIQUE constraints can be created in a table.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    UNIQUE constraints can be named explicitly.
                </li>

                <li>
                    UNIQUE constraints can be added using ALTER TABLE.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->
    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned how the UNIQUE constraint
                prevents duplicate values, how to create it during
                table creation and how to add it to an existing table.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


